// Worker code is TypeScript and the runtime here is plain Node, so `npm test`
// compiles the modules under test to .test-build/ and runs node:test over the
// output. No bundler, and no workerd.
//
// WHAT IT COMPILES IS A GLOB — `tests/**` and `src/**`, and no per-file
// inventory (#595). It used to be a hand-kept list of seventy-odd source paths,
// which was three things at once and honest about none of them: a build input,
// a claim that "only what the tests import gets compiled", and a de-facto
// purity register. All three had already failed. The list never governed what
// was compiled — `tsc` follows imports, so 140 source files were on this build
// while seventy were named — and a module's absence from it read as a boundary
// when it was usually an omission nobody had noticed. Adding a module now means
// adding a module.
//
// The Workers types sit BESIDE the Node ones (rather than instead of, as in
// tsconfig.json) since `tests/notion-write.test.ts`: a mocked-fetch test over a
// real integration reaches `Env`, and `Env` names the Durable Object and KV
// binding types. `skipLibCheck` keeps the two global sets from arguing. So
// naming `Env`, or any Workers type, does not keep a module off this compile
// and has not for some time — which is why no module's header should give it as
// the reason the module exists (#595 swept the ones that did).
//
// THE BOUNDARY THAT HOLDS IS THE RUNTIME ONE. A test here runs on Node, so a
// module that needs a real `fetch`, a binding call or a Workers global to do
// its work is one this suite can compile and cannot DRIVE; a module free of
// them can be driven. That is a property of the module, stated in the module,
// and demonstrated by the test that imports it — not a line in this file.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "module": "NodeNext",
    "moduleResolution": "NodeNext",
    "types": ["node", "@cloudflare/workers-types"],
    "noEmit": false,
    "outDir": ".test-build"
  },
  // tests/workerd/ is the ONE suite that needs Cloudflare's runtime: vitest
  // compiles it itself (vitest.workerd.config.mts, `npm run test:workerd`), and
  // it imports `cloudflare:test`, a specifier that resolves under vitest's
  // Workers pool and not under NodeNext here. It stays the only carve-out, and
  // it is a RUNTIME one — the same distinction the header draws.
  "exclude": ["tests/workerd/**"],
  "include": ["tests/**/*.ts", "src/**/*.ts"]
}
